### What the shield layers are looking for

All three OSM-based shield layers (OSM Bright, OSM Liberty and the “transportation_name” layer that ships with the OpenMapTiles style) build the **`icon-image`** value from two feature properties:

```json
"icon-image": "{network}_{ref_length}"
```

* `network` – a string that tells you which family of shields to use.  
  The basemap you are using (the default OpenMapTiles style) uses the following values:

| network value | What it means |
|---------------|----------------|
| `us-interstate` | Interstate highways (I-X) |
| `us-highway`    | US-highways (US X) |
| `us-state`      | State highways (CA X, TX X, …) |
| `road`          | All other roads (e.g. “A-X”, “B-X”, “M-X”, …) |

* `ref_length` – the length of the `ref` string (the number of characters).  
  The style creates a separate badge image for each possible length, because the badge is a fixed-size PNG that the text is drawn on top of.

Putting those two together gives the **exact sprite-image IDs** that the style will try to fetch:

| Network | `ref_length` values that exist in the *official* OSM Bright sprite |
|---------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `us-interstate` | `us-interstate_1`, `us-interstate_2`, `us-interstate_3` |
| `us-highway`    | `us-highway_1`, `us-highway_2`, `us-highway_3` |
| `us-state`      | `us-state_1`, `us-state_2`, `us-state_3`, `us-state_4`, `us-state_5`, `us-state_6` |
| `road`          | `road_1`, `road_2`, `road_3`, `road_4`, `road_5`, `road_6` |

> **Note** – OSM Liberty ships a slightly larger set for `us-interstate` (up to `_5`).  
> The table above reflects the *minimum* set you need for the standard OpenMapTiles style.

If a feature’s `ref_length` is larger than the highest badge that exists in the sprite, the style still asks for the image (e.g. `us-interstate_4`) and you will see a bare number because the image cannot be found.

---

## How to get the badges to appear **without touching the style layers**

You have three practical ways to make those images available to the map. All of them keep the existing `icon-image` expression (`{network}_{ref_length}`) untouched.

### 1. Point the style at a sprite that already contains the needed IDs

The easiest solution is to use the **official OpenMapTiles sprite** that ships with the OSM Bright / OSM Liberty styles:

```json
{
  "sprite": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite"
}
```

or, if you are using the Liberty style:

```json
{
  "sprite": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-liberty-gl-style/sprite"
}
```

Both URLs are **extension-less** base URLs; MapLibre will request the `.json` and `.png` (and `@2x` variants) automatically.

If you already have a custom sprite for your own icons, you can **add a second sheet** (MapLibre ≥ 3.0) instead of replacing the whole one:

```json
{
  "sprite": [
    { "id": "default", "url": "https://mydomain.com/my-custom-sprite" },
    { "id": "osm-shields", "url": "https://openmaptiles.github.io/osm-bright-gl-style/sprite" }
  ]
}
```

Then reference the shield images with the `id` prefix:

```json
"icon-image": "osm-shields:{network}_{ref_length}"
```

Because the `default` sheet is still the first entry, all your existing custom icons keep working without any prefix change.

### 2. Add the missing badge images at runtime with `addImage`

If you cannot change the `sprite` URL (e.g. you are locked into a hosted style) you can **register the missing images yourself** after the style has loaded:

```js
map.on('load', async () => {
  const badgeNames = [
    // us-interstate
    'us-interstate_1','us-interstate_2','us-interstate_3',
    // us-highway
    'us-highway_1','us-highway_2','us-highway_3',
    // us-state
    'us-state_1','us-state_2','us-state_3','us-state_4','us-state_5','us-state_6',
    // generic road
    'road_1','road_2','road_3','road_4','road_5','road_6'
  ];

  for (const name of badgeNames) {
    if (!map.hasImage(name)) {
      // You can host the PNGs yourself (any CDN, S3, etc.)
      const url = `https://mycdn.example.com/shields/${name}.png`;
      const { data } = await map.loadImage(url);
      map.addImage(name, data);
    }
  }
});
```

* Each badge must be a **PNG (or WebP/JPEG) raster**; SVGs are not accepted by `addImage` unless you rasterise them yourself first.
* The image **must not be marked `sdf: true`** – the shield PNGs are ordinary bitmaps, not signed-distance-field icons.
* Once the images are added, the existing `icon-image` expression (`{network}_{ref_length}`) will automatically start finding them and the badges will appear.

### 3. Build and host your own sprite that contains the shield images

If you already have a sprite for your own icons and want the shields to live in the same sheet, you can **merge the shield PNGs into your sprite** and host the combined sprite yourself.

1. **Download the official shield PNGs** (the 22 × 22-pixel PNGs used by OSM Bright). They are available in the OpenMapTiles repo under `sprites/`.  
   Example: `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/openmaptiles/osm-bright-gl-style/master/sprites/road_1.png`

2. **Add them to your own SVG source directory** (or copy the PNGs directly into a folder that `spritezero`/`spreet`/`martin` can read).

3. **Run a sprite builder** (e.g. `spritezero`):

   ```bash
   npx spritezero my-sprite ./icons   # ./icons contains all PNGs (your own + the shield PNGs)
   ```

   This produces `my-sprite.png` and `my-sprite.json`.

4. **Host the two files** (and the `@2x` variants if you need high-DPI support) on a static host you control.

5. **Point the style’s `sprite` property** at the new base URL:

   ```json
   { "sprite": "https://mycdn.example.com/my-sprite" }
   ```

Because the sprite now contains the exact IDs (`us-interstate_1`, …, `road_6`) the style will find them automatically.

---

## Quick checklist – “Why am I still seeing numbers only?”

| ✅ Check | What to do |
|--------|------------|
| **Sprite URL is correct** | Verify the style’s `sprite` points to a base URL **without** `.json` or `.png`. Open `…/sprite.json` in a browser – you should see a JSON object with keys like `"us-interstate_2"` |
| **Badge IDs exist in the JSON** | Search the JSON for the exact key you need (`us-interstate_3`, `road_5`, …). If it’s missing, you need one of the three solutions above. |
| **CORS is allowed** | The sprite PNG/JSON must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). Otherwise the map will log `Image "<id>" could not be loaded`. |
| **`ref_length` never exceeds the highest badge** | If you have a route like `I-405` (`ref_length = 3`) but the sprite only goes up to `_2`, the style will ask for `us-interstate_3` → missing. Use a sprite that includes up to the longest ref you expect, or add a stretchable badge (see below). |
| **Optional: stretchable badges** | If you want a *single* badge image to work for any length, create a badge with `stretchX`/`stretchY` and `content` in the sprite JSON, then set `icon-text-fit: "both"` (or `"width"`). This is a style edit, but you can also add a pre-stretched badge via `addImage` with the same metadata. |

---

## Bonus: Using a single stretchable badge (no style edit)

If you cannot edit the style but you *can* add images at runtime, you can **replace the whole set of badge images with one stretchable version**:

```js
map.on('load', async () => {
  const url = 'https://mycdn.example.com/shields/road_stretchable.png';
  const { data } = await map.loadImage(url);
  // The metadata mirrors what a sprite entry would contain:
  const metadata = {
    // The badge is 22 px high, 22 px wide, but we allow the middle 10 px to stretch
    stretchX: [[10, 12]],
    stretchY: [[0, 22]],
    // The text must stay inside the central 10 px region
    content: [10, 0, 12, 22],
    // Tell the renderer that the image is *not* an SDF
    sdf: false
  };
  // Register the same name for every possible badge ID
  const ids = [
    'us-interstate_1','us-interstate_2','us-interstate_3',
    'us-highway_1','us-highway_2','us-highway_3',
    'us-state_1','us-state_2','us-state_3','us-state_4','us-state_5','us-state_6',
    'road_1','road_2','road_3','road_4','road_5','road_6'
  ];
  ids.forEach(id => {
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) map.addImage(id, data, metadata);
  });
});
```

Because the image is registered under **every** expected ID, the existing `{network}_{ref_length}` expression resolves to the same stretchable badge, and the numbers will be drawn on a correctly-sized background without any layer changes.

---

### TL;DR – What you need to do

1. **Make sure the style can reach a sprite that contains the IDs**  
   `us-interstate_1 … us-interstate_3`  
   `us-highway_1 … us-highway_3`  
   `us-state_1 … us-state_6`  
   `road_1 … road_6`

2. **If you cannot change the `sprite` URL**, add those PNGs at runtime with `map.addImage` (or `map.addSprite` for a whole second sheet).

3. **Alternatively**, rebuild your own sprite that merges your custom icons **and** the shield PNGs, host it, and point `sprite` at it.

Once the sprite (or runtime images) contains the exact IDs above, the shield badges will appear automatically, and you won’t need to touch any layer definitions. Happy mapping!
